window.AUTH=window.AUTH||{};window.AUTH["21-1576"]={"d":"21-1576","id":"2022/21-1576","n":"Smith v. United States","briefs":[{"k":"petitioner","label":"Brief of petitioner Timothy J. Smith","date":"2023-01-27","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/21/21-1576/253367/20230127165939742_2023-01-27%20No.%2021-1576%20-%20Smith%20Merits%20Brief.pdf","side":"pet","n":76},{"k":"respondent","label":"Brief of respondent United States","date":"2023-02-27","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/21/21-1576/255687/20230227163037534_21-1576bsUnitedStates.pdf","side":"res","n":79},{"k":"reply","label":"Reply of petitioner Timothy J. Smith","date":"2023-03-17","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/21/21-1576/259298/20230317122512052_2023-03-17%20Smith%20No.%2021-1576%20-%20Merits%20Reply%20Brief.pdf","side":"pet","n":31}],"opinion":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/22pdf/599us1r37_8m59.pdf","tr":true,"items":[{"t":"case","label":"Baldeo v. United States","cite":"2018 WL 1116570","y":2018,"court":"S.D.N.Y. Feb. 26","sum":"Noting that “the jury was instructed that if it could not find venue in SDNY, it must acquit”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"38"}},{"t":"case","label":"Barker v. Wingo","cite":"407 U.S. 514","y":1972,"id":"1971/71-5255","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that the right to a speedy trial differs from other constitutionally guaranteed rights because it is often more in the interest of society and the justice system as a whole than it is in the interest of the accused. Additionally, there is no way to create a firm distinction between what is and is not a speedy trial, since the circumstances surrounding each trial are unique.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"28, 29","1":"27, 28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bell v. Hood","cite":"327 U.S. 678","y":1946,"id":"scdb/1945-042","sc":1,"sum":"1. Where the complaint seeks recovery squarely on the ground of violation of plaintiffs' rights under the Fourth and Fifth Amendments, a federal district court has jurisdiction of a suit against agents of the Federal Government to recover damages in excess of $3,000 alleged to have been suffered by the plaintiffs as a result of such violations-even though neither the Constitution nor the Congress has provided for …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Betterman v. Montana","cite":"578 U.S. 437","y":2016,"id":"2015/14-1457","sc":1,"sum":"The Court determined that the Sixth Amendment’s guarantee of a speedy trial does not extend past a guilty verdict or guilty plea.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"28, 29","1":"13","2":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Burks v. United States","cite":"437 U.S. 1","y":1978,"id":"1977/76-6528","sc":1,"op":7,"ar":19,"sum":"The Double Jeopardy Clause of the Fifth Amendment precludes a second trial once the reviewing court has found the evidence insufficient to sustain the jury's verdict of guilty, and the only \"just\" remedy available for that court under 28 U. S. C. § 2106 is the entry of a judgment of acquittal.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"39","1":"20, 32","2":"3, 20, 21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Commonwealth v. Call","cite":"38 Mass. 509","y":1839,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Mass./38/509/","op":1,"by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Conley v. State","cite":"11 S.E. 659","y":1890,"court":"Ga.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.E./11/659/","by":{"0":"35"}},{"t":"case","label":"Crawford v. Washington","cite":"541 U.S. 36","y":2004,"id":"2003/02-9410","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court sided with Crawford and ruled that the Sixth Amendment's Confrontation Clause gives defendants the right to confront witnesses and cross-examine their testimony. This includes testimony police gather. The Court reasoned that the Framers intended the Confrontation Clause to prohibit out-of-court testimony as evidence against defendants.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"20, 27","2":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"Evans v. Michigan","cite":"568 U.S. 313","y":2013,"id":"2012/11-1327","sc":1,"op":2,"ar":2,"sum":"The Court held that the Double Jeopardy clause bars a second trial even if the acquittal of the first trial was done in error.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"35, 39, 44","1":"13","2":"16, 20, 21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Page(s) Fong Foo v. United States","cite":"369 U.S. 141","y":1962,"id":"1961/64","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that vacating the acquittal and granting a new trial violated petitioners’ constitutional protection against double jeopardy. Justice John M. Harlan concurred, agreeing that double jeopardy barred a second trial, because the trial court did not base its decision solely on the Assistant U.S. Attorney’s misconduct.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"39","2":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Green v. United States","cite":"309 F.2d 852","y":1962,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/309/852/","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Haney v. Burgess","cite":"799 F.2d 661","y":1986,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/799/661/","by":{"0":"16, 17, 41, 43","1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hyde v. United States","cite":"225 U.S. 347","y":1912,"id":"scdb/1911-076","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"In this ease the defendant applied for a writ of certiorari and the Attorney General assented'to granting it on the ground that the determination of the case depends upon the principles of law governing conspiracy and it is of vital importance to the United States, as well as its citizens, to have those principles settled by this court.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"10, 45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lawless v. State","cite":"72 Tenn. 173","y":1879,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Tenn./72/173/","sum":"Holding that defendant was entitled to jury instructions charging “that the jury must be satisfied ‘beyond a reasonable doubt’ by the proof that the crime was committed in the proper county before they can convict”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Maine v. Moulton","cite":"474 U.S. 159","y":1985,"id":"1985/84-786","sc":1,"sum":"Respondent's Sixth Amendment right to the assistance of counsel was violated by the admission at trial of incriminating statements made by him to Colson after indictment and at the meeting of the two to plan defense strategy for the upcoming trial.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Myers v. United States","cite":"272 U.S. 52","y":1926,"id":"1900-1940/272us52","sc":1,"sum":"After tracing legislative debate of the First Congress in 1789 which dealt with the interpretation of the President's appointment power, Chief Justice Taft concluded that the power to remove appointed officers is vested in the President alone. According to Taft, to deny the President that power would not allow him to \"discharge his own constitutional duty of seeing that the laws be faithfully executed.\".","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"NLRB v. Noel Canning","cite":"573 U.S. 513","y":2014,"id":"2013/12-1281","sc":1,"sum":"A case in which the Court further defined the limitations on the presidential powers granted by the Recess Appointments Clause.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"21, 31"}},{"t":"case","label":"People v. Gleason","cite":"1 Nev. 174","y":1865,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Nev./1/174/","by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"People v. Harris","cite":"4 Denio 150","y":1847,"court":"N.Y. Sup. Ct.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Denio/4/150/","by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ramos v. Louisiana","cite":"140 S. Ct. 1390","y":2020,"id":"2019/18-5924","sc":1,"op":3,"sum":"The Court held that the Fourteenth Amendment fully incorporates the Sixth Amendment’s guarantee of a unanimous verdict against the states.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"26, 31","1":"23","2":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Reed v. Ross","cite":"468 U.S. 1","y":1984,"id":"1983/83-218","sc":1,"sum":"Respondent had \"cause\" for failing to raise the Mullaney issue on appeal from his conviction.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Seila Law LLC v. Consumer Financial Protection Bureau","cite":"140 S. Ct. 2183","y":2020,"id":"2019/19-7","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the vesting of substantial executive authority in the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, an independent agency led by a single director, violates the separation of powers, but the statutory provision creating that violation, 12 U.S.C. § 5491(c)(3) is severable from the Dodd-Frank Act.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Smith v. Massachusetts","cite":"543 U.S. 462","y":2005,"id":"2004/03-8661","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that the double jeopardy clause barred the judge from reconsidering the acquittal on the firearm charge later in the trial. Smith was subjected to successive prosecutions because, according to Court precedent, the midtrial ruling was an acquittal and the submission of the count to the jury was a further proceeding dealing with guilt or innocence.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"39","2":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"State v. George","cite":"8 Rob. 535","y":1844,"court":"La.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Rob./8/535/","op":1,"sum":"Noting that, if the jury returned a special verdict, failure to prove venue would not bar reprosecution in the proper parish because the first tribunal “would have been without jurisdiction” under state statutes.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"36"}},{"t":"case","label":"State v. Rhoda","cite":"23 Ark. 156","y":1861,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ark./23/156/","by":{"0":"34, 36"}},{"t":"case","label":"Stevenson v. Lewis","cite":"384 F.3d 1069","y":2004,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/384/1069/","by":{"0":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Strunk v. United States","cite":"412 U.S. 434","y":1973,"id":"1972/72-5521","sc":1,"sum":"In this case, the only question for review is the propriety of the remedy fashioned by the Court of Appeals..In light. of the policies underlying the right to a speedy trial, dismissal must remain, as noted in Barker v. Wingo, 407 U. S. 514, 522, \"the only possible remedy\" for deprivation 'of this constitutional right. Pp. 435-440. 467 F. 2d 969. reversed and remanded. BURGER, C. J., wiote the opinion for a …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"4, 18, 24, 28, 29, 30","1":"27","2":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Travis v. United States","cite":"364 U.S. 631","y":1961,"id":"1960/10","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"Holding that prosecution was proper only in Washington, D. C., rather than in the defendant's residence in Colorado.","sumSrc":"opinion","by":{"0":"9, 42"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Ball","cite":"163 U.S. 662","y":1896,"id":"scdb/1895-270","sc":1,"ar":6,"sum":"A general verdict of acquittal, in a court having jurisdiction of the canse and of the defendant, upon the issue of not guilty to an indictment undertaking to charge murder, and not objected to before verdict as insufficient In that respect, Is a bar to a subsequent indictment against him for the same killing.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"35","1":"17, 32","2":"2, 14, 15"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Black Cloud","cite":"590 F.2d 270","y":1979,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/590/270/","op":2,"by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Bowens","cite":"224 F.3d 302","y":2000,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/224/302/","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Britton","cite":"24 F. Cas. 1239","y":1822,"court":"C.C.D. Mass.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Cas./24/1239/","by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Burr","cite":"25 F. Cas. 187","y":1807,"court":"C.C.D. Va.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Cas./25/187/","by":{"0":"21, 33, 36","1":"16","2":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Cabrales","cite":"524 U.S. 1","y":1998,"id":"1997/97-643","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that Missouri is not a place of proper venue for the money-laundering offenses with which Cabrales is charged.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"5, 7, 15, 21","2":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Calderon","cite":"243 F.3d 587","y":2001,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/243/587/","by":{"0":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Cores","cite":"356 U.S. 405","y":1958,"id":"1957/455","sc":1,"sum":"..An alien crewman*who willfully remains in the United States in excess of the 29 days allowed by hisonditional landing permit, in violation of § 252 c) of the Immigration and Nationaliiy Act, is guilty of a cdntinuing offense which may be proiecuted in any. district where he is found, even though it is not tIhe district where he was present when his permit expired. Pp. 405-410. Reversed and remanded.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"9, 25"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Davis","cite":"666 F.2d 195","y":1982,"court":"5th Cir. Unit B","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/666/195/","sum":"Finding insufficient evidence of venue for possession of drugs where defendants did not “actually or constructively” possess the drugs in the Middle District of Georgia and drugs “never physically entered” that district.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"41, 46"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Fakih","cite":"2006 WL 1997479","y":2006,"court":"S.D.N.Y. July 18","by":{"0":"38"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Georgacarakos","cite":"988 F.2d 1289","y":1993,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/988/1289/","by":{"0":"38"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Greene","cite":"995 F.2d 793","y":1993,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/995/793/","by":{"0":"15","1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Hamilton","cite":"587 F.3d 1199","y":2009,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/587/1199/","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Johnson","cite":"323 U.S. 273","y":1944,"id":"scdb/1944-007","sc":1,"sum":"1. A prosecution for using the mails for sending dentures in violation of the Federal Denture Act-which Act contains no specific provision relative to the venue of prosecutions thereunder-can not be had in the district to which the dentures were sent but only in the district from which they were sent.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"9, 10, 18, 25","2":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Kaytso","cite":"868 F.2d 1020","y":1988,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/868/1020/","by":{"0":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Lam Kwong-Wah","cite":"924 F.2d 298","y":1991,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/924/298/","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Marion","cite":"404 U.S. 307","y":1971,"id":"1971/70-19","sc":1,"sum":"Appellees, claiming that the Government had known of the crimes with which they were charged, the circumstances of the crimes, and appellees' identities for over three years before they were indicted, moved to dismiss on the ground that the indictment was returned \"an unreasonably oppressive and unjustifiable time after the alleged offenses,\" and that the delay deprived them of rights to due process of law and a …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Martin Linen Supply Co.","cite":"430 U.S. 564","y":1977,"id":"1976/76-120","sc":1,"op":3,"sum":"The Double Jeopardy Clause bars appellate 'review and retrial following a judgment of acquittal entered under Rule 29 (c).","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"44","2":"2, 16, 21"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Massa","cite":"686 F.2d 526","y":1982,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/686/526/","by":{"0":"37, 38"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Maxwell","cite":"2021 WL 5999410","y":2021,"court":"S.D.N.Y. Dec. 19","op":1,"by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Miller","cite":"111 F.3d 747","y":1997,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/111/747/","by":{"0":"38"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Moran-Garcia","cite":"966 F.3d 966","y":2020,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/966/966/","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Morrison","cite":"449 U.S. 361","y":1981,"id":"1980/79-395","sc":1,"op":3,"sum":"For sake of argument, the Court granted that Morrison's Sixth Amendment right to counsel had been violated, but held that this did not merit the dismissal of all charges since the conduct of the agents did not prejudice the outcome of the trial. The Court maintained that Sixth Amendment violations should not be remedied beyond the scope of the harm they inflicted upon the defendant.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"24","1":"21-24, 27, 31, 35","2":"6, 10, 11"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Palma-Ruedas","cite":"121 F.3d 841","y":1997,"court":"3d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/121/841/","sum":"Explaining that venue right serves “to deter governmental abuses of power”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"7, 9, 10, 27, 42"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Peoni","cite":"100 F.2d 401","y":1938,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/100/401/","by":{"0":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Perez","cite":"280 F.3d 318","y":2002,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/280/318/","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Petlechkov","cite":"922 F.3d 762","y":2019,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/922/762/","by":{"0":"37, 43","1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Plympton","cite":"27 F. Cas. 578","y":1833,"court":"C.C.D.D.C.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Cas./27/578/","op":1,"by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Rodriguez-Moreno","cite":"526 U.S. 275","y":1999,"id":"1998/97-1139","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"The Court held that \"[v]enue in a prosecution for using or carrying a firearm 'during and in relation to any crime of violence' in violation of section 924(c)(1) is proper in any district where the crime of violence was committed.\" Justice Thomas wrote for the Court that, \"A kidnapping...does not end until the victim is free.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"13","1":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Romans","cite":"823 F.3d 299","y":2016,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/823/299/","by":{"0":"22, 46"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Scott","cite":"270 F.3d 30","y":2001,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/270/30/","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Scott","cite":"437 U.S. 82","y":1978,"id":"1977/76-1382","sc":1,"op":6,"sum":"Where a defendant himself seeks to have his trial terminated without any submission to either judge or jury as to his guilt or innocence, an appeal by the Government from his successful effort to do so does not offend the Double Jeopardy Clause, and hence is not barred by 18 U. S. C. § 3731 (1976 ed.). United States v. Jenkins, supra, overruled.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"33, 44","1":"20, 32, 33","2":"21, 22"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Smith","cite":"641 F.3d 1200","y":2011,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/641/1200/","sum":"Finding venue lacking where government brought charges in the Western District of Oklahoma based on allegedly false statements made by the defendant at his home in Minnesota.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Snipes","cite":"611 F.3d 855","y":2010,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/611/855/","by":{"0":"37, 38, 39"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Strain","cite":"396 F.3d 689","y":2005,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/396/689/","sum":"Explaining that venue would plainly have been proper in the District of New Mexico, but finding insufficient evidence of venue in the Western District of Texas, where defendant merely received a phone call.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Strain","cite":"407 F.3d 379","y":2005,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/407/379/","by":{"0":"15, 38","1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Tang Yuik","cite":"885 F.3d 57","y":2018,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/885/57/","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Wilson","cite":"28 F. Cas. 699","y":1830,"court":"C.C.E.D. Pa.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Cas./28/699/","by":{"0":"33, 34, 35"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Wright","cite":"28 F. Cas. 790","y":1822,"court":"C.C.D.D.C.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Cas./28/790/","by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Wilkett v. United States","cite":"655 F.2d 1007","y":1981,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/655/1007/","by":{"0":"43","1":"18"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment VI","clauses":[["sixth-trial","Speedy and Public Trial by Jury"],["confrontation","Confrontation Clause"],["counsel","Right to Counsel"]],"text":"In all criminal prosecutions, the accused shall enjoy the right to a speedy and public trial, by an impartial jury of the State and district wherein the crime shall have been committed, which district shall have been previously ascertained by law, and to be informed of the nature and cause of the accusation; to be confronted with the witnesses against him; to have compulsory process for obtaining witnesses in his favor, and to have the Assistance of Counsel for his defence.","by":{"0":"2, 9, 18, 20, 22, 24, 28","1":"2, 5, 8, 11, 21-27, 30"}},{"t":"const","label":"Article III, Section 2, Clause 3","text":"The Trial of all Crimes, except in Cases of Impeachment, shall be by Jury; and such Trial shall be held in the State where the said Crimes shall have been committed; but when not committed within any State, the Trial shall be at such Place or Places as the Congress may by Law have directed.","by":{"0":"1, 7, 17, 20, 24"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 875","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/875","subs":["(d)"],"stext":{"head":"§875. Interstate communications","paras":[[0,"(a) Whoever transmits in interstate or foreign commerce any communication containing any demand or request for a ransom or reward for the release of any kidnapped person, shall be fined under this title or imprisoned not more than twenty years, or both."],[0,"(b) Whoever, with intent to extort from any person, firm, association, or corporation, any money or other thing of value, transmits in interstate or foreign commerce any communication containing any threat to kidnap any person or any threat to injure the person of another, shall be fined under this title or imprisoned not more than twenty years, or both."],[0,"(c) Whoever transmits in interstate or foreign commerce any communication containing any threat to kidnap any person or any threat to injure the person of another, shall be fined under this title or imprisoned not more than five years, or both."],[0,"(d) Whoever, with intent to extort from any person, firm, association, or corporation, any money or other thing of value, transmits in interstate or foreign commerce any communication containing any threat to injure the property or reputation of the addressee or of another or the reputation of a deceased person or any threat to accuse the addressee or any other person of a crime, shall be fined under this title or imprisoned not more than two years, or both."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/18/875?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§875. Interstate communications","tname":"Crimes and Criminal Procedure","by":{"0":"10","1":"3, 4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 1030","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1030","subs":["(a)(1)","(a)(2)(C)","(c)(2)(B)(iii)"],"stext":{"head":"§1030. Fraud and related activity in connection with computers","paras":[[0,"(a) Whoever—"],[1,"(1) having knowingly accessed a computer without authorization or exceeding authorized access, and by means of such conduct having obtained information that has been determined by the United States Government pursuant to an Executive order or statute to require protection against unauthorized disclosure for reasons of national defense or foreign relations, or any restricted data, as defined in paragraph y. of section 11 of the Atomic Energy Act of 1954, with reason to believe that such information so obtained could be used to the injury of the United States, or to the advantage of any foreign nation willfully communicates, delivers, transmits, or causes to be communicated, delivered, or transmitted, or attempts to communicate, deliver, transmit or cause to be communicated, delivered, or transmitted the same to any person not entitled to receive it, or willfully retains the same and fails to deliver it to the officer or employee of the United States entitled to receive it;"],[1,"(2) intentionally accesses a computer without authorization or exceeds authorized access, and thereby obtains—"],[2,"(A) information contained in a financial record of a financial institution, or of a card issuer as defined in section 1602(n) 1 of title 15, or contained in a file of a consumer reporting agency on a consumer, as such terms are defined in the Fair Credit Reporting Act (15 U.S.C. 1681 et seq.);"],[2,"(B) information from any department or agency of the United States; or"],[2,"(C) information from any protected computer;"],[1,"(3) intentionally, without authorization to access any nonpublic computer of a department or agency of the United States, accesses such a computer of that department or agency that is exclusively for the use of the Government of the United States or, in the case of a computer not exclusively for such use, is used by or for the Government of the United States and such conduct affects that use by or for the Government of the United States;"],[1,"(4) knowingly and with intent to defraud, accesses a protected computer without authorization, or exceeds authorized access, and by means of such conduct furthers the intended fraud and obtains anything of value, unless the object of the fraud and the thing obtained consists only of the use of the computer and the value of such use is not more than $5,000 in any 1-year period;"],[1,"(5)(A) knowingly causes the transmission of a program, information, code, or command, and as a result of such conduct, intentionally causes damage without authorization, to a protected computer;"],[1,"(B) intentionally accesses a protected computer without authorization, and as a result of such conduct, recklessly causes damage; or"],[1,"(C) intentionally accesses a protected computer without authorization, and as a result of such conduct, causes damage and loss.2"],[1,"(6) knowingly and with intent to defraud traffics (as defined in section 1029) in any password or similar information through which a computer may be accessed without authorization, if—"],[2,"(A) such trafficking affects interstate or foreign commerce; or"],[2,"(B) such computer is used by or for the Government of the United States; 3"],[1,"(7) with intent to extort from any person any money or other thing of value, transmits in interstate or foreign commerce any communication containing any—"],[2,"(A) threat to cause damage to a protected computer;"],[2,"(B) threat to obtain information from a protected computer without authorization or in excess of authorization or to impair the confidentiality of information obtained from a protected computer without authorization or by exceeding authorized access; or"],[2,"(C) demand or request for money or other thing of value in relation to damage to a protected computer, where such damage was caused to facilitate the extortion;"],[0,"shall be punished as provided in subsection (c) of this section."],[0,"(b) Whoever conspires to commit or attempts to commit an offense under subsection (a) of this section shall be punished as provided in subsection (c) of this section."],[0,"(c) The punishment for an offense under subsection (a) or (b) of this section is—"],[1,"(1)(A) a fine under this title or imprisonment for not more than ten years, or both, in the case of an offense under subsection (a)(1) of this section which does not occur after a conviction for another offense under this section, or an attempt to commit an offense punishable under this subparagraph; and"],[1,"(B) a fine under this title or imprisonment for not more than twenty years, or both, in the case of an offense under subsection (a)(1) of this section which occurs after a conviction for another offense under this section, or an attempt to commit an offense punishable under this subparagraph;"],[1,"(2)(A) except as provided in subparagraph (B), a fine under this title or imprisonment for not more than one year, or both, in the case of an offense under subsection (a)(2), (a)(3), or (a)(6) of this section which does not occur after a conviction for another offense under this section, or an attempt to commit an offense punishable under this subparagraph;"],[1,"(B) a fine under this title or imprisonment for not more than 5 years, or both, in the case of an offense under subsection (a)(2), or an attempt to commit an offense punishable under this subparagraph, if—"],[2,"(i) the offense was committed for purposes of commercial advantage or private financial gain;"],[2,"(ii) the offense was committed in furtherance of any criminal or tortious act in violation of the Constitution or laws of the United States or of any State; or"],[2,"(iii) the value of the information obtained exceeds $5,000; and"],[1,"(C) a fine under this title or imprisonment for not more than ten years, or both, in the case of an offense under subsection (a)(2), (a)(3) or (a)(6) of this section which occurs after a conviction for another offense under this section, or an attempt to commit an offense punishable under this subparagraph;"],[1,"(3)(A) a fine under this title or imprisonment for not more than five years, or both, in the case of an offense under subsection (a)(4) or (a)(7) of this section which does not occur after a conviction for another offense under this section, or an attempt to commit an offense punishable under this subparagraph; and"],[1,"(B) a fine under this title or imprisonment for not more than ten years, or both, in the case of an offense under subsection (a)(4),4 or (a)(7) of this section which occurs after a conviction for another offense under this section, or an attempt to commit an offense punishable under this subparagraph;"],[1,"(4)(A) except as provided in subparagraphs (E) and (F), a fine under this title, imprisonment for not more than 5 years, or both, in the case of—"],[2,"(i) an offense under subsection (a)(5)(B), which does not occur after a conviction for another offense under this section, if the offense caused (or, in the case of an attempted offense, would, if completed, have caused)—"],[3,"(I) loss to 1 or more persons during any 1-year period (and, for purposes of an investigation, prosecution, or other proceeding brought by the United States only, loss resulting from a related course of conduct affecting 1 or more other protected computers) aggregating at least $5,000 in value;"],[3,"(II) the modification or impairment, or potential modification or impairment, of the medical examination, diagnosis, treatment, or care of 1 or more individuals;"],[3,"(III) physical injury to any person;"],[3,"(IV) a threat to public health or safety;"],[3,"(V) damage affecting a computer used by or for an entity of the United States Government in furtherance of the administration of justice, national defense, or national security; or"],[3,"(VI) damage affecting 10 or more protected computers during any 1-year period; or"],[2,"(ii) an attempt to commit an offense punishable under this subparagraph;"],[1,"(B) except as provided in subparagraphs (E) and (F), a fine under this title, imprisonment for not more than 10 years, or both, in the case of—"],[2,"(i) an offense under subsection (a)(5)(A), which does not occur after a conviction for another offense under this section, if the offense caused (or, in the case of an attempted offense, would, if completed, have caused) a harm provided in subclauses (I) through (VI) of subparagraph (A)(i); or"],[2,"(ii) an attempt to commit an offense punishable under this subparagraph;"],[1,"(C) except as provided in subparagraphs (E) and (F), a fine under this title, imprisonment for not more than 20 years, or both, in the case of—"],[2,"(i) an offense or an attempt to commit an offense under subparagraphs (A) or (B) of subsection (a)(5) that occurs after a conviction for another offense under this section; or"],[2,"(ii) an attempt to commit an offense punishable under this subparagraph;"],[1,"(D) a fine under this title, imprisonment for not more than 10 years, or both, in the case of—"],[2,"(i) an offense or an attempt to commit an offense under subsection (a)(5)(C) that occurs after a conviction for another offense under this section; or"],[2,"(ii) an attempt to commit an offense punishable under this subparagraph;"],[1,"(E) if the offender attempts to cause or knowingly or recklessly causes serious bodily injury from conduct in violation of subsection (a)(5)(A), a fine under this title, imprisonment for not more than 20 years, or both;"]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/18/1030?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§1030. Fraud and related activity in connection with computers","tname":"Crimes and Criminal Procedure","by":{"0":"10, 10, 10","1":"4, 4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1254","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1254","subs":["(1)"],"by":{"0":"1"}},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. Crim. P. 18","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcrmp/rule_18","by":{"0":"2, 13","1":"2, 5, 11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Albright v. Oliver","cite":"510 U.S. 266","y":1994,"id":"1993/92-833","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled that Section 1983 relief for prosecution without probable cause is only valid if the prosecuted party claims Fourth Amendment (pretrial rights) violations.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Alderman v. United States","cite":"394 U.S. 165","y":1969,"id":"1967/133","sc":1,"sum":"In a majority opinion speaking for himself and four other members of the Court, Justice Byron R. White held that \"conversations as well as property are excludable from the criminal trial when they are found to be the fruits of an illegal invasion of the home.\" Moreover, continued White, \"surveillance records as to which any petitioner has standing to object should be turned over to him without being screened in camera by the trial judge.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"(III) Cases—Continued: Ballew v. Georgia","cite":"435 U.S. 223","y":1978,"id":"1977/76-761","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court found that a trial by jury of less than six members violated the accused's right to a trial by jury as protected by the Sixth and Fourteenth Amendments. Justice Blackmun reasoned that small juries foster poor group deliberation.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Batson v. Kentucky","cite":"476 U.S. 79","y":1986,"id":"1985/84-6263","sc":1,"sum":"The Court found that the prosecutor's actions violated the Sixth and Fourteenth Amendments of the Constitution.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"23, 26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bravo-Fernandez v. United States","cite":"580 U.S. 5","y":2016,"id":"2016/15-537","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court decided that the Double Jeopardy Clause does not bar retrial when a jury returns an inconsistent verdict of conviction and acquittal and the convictions are later vacated for a completely separate legal error.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"32, 35"}},{"t":"case","label":"Campbell v. People","cite":"109 Ill. 565","y":1884,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ill./109/565/","by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cutter v. Wilkinson","cite":"544 U.S. 709","y":2005,"id":"2004/03-9877","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that, on its face, RLUIPA made an accommodation allowed by the First Amendment. The Court reasoned that the law was an effort to alleviate the \"government-created burden\" on religious exercise that prisoners faced. Nor did section three discriminate between mainstream and non-mainstream religions.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Derry v. Commonwealth","cite":"274 S.W.3d 439","y":2008,"court":"Ky.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.W.3d/274/439/","by":{"1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Edwards v. Arizona","cite":"451 U.S. 477","y":1981,"id":"1980/79-5269","sc":1,"sum":"The use of petitioner's confession against him at his trial violated his right under the Fifth and Fourteenth Amendments to have counsel present during custodial interrogation, as declared in Miranda, supra. Having exercised his right on January 19 to have counsel present during interrogation, petitioner did not validly waive that right on the 20th.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Fabian v. State","cite":"284 So. 2d 55","y":1973,"court":"Miss.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/So.%202d/284/55/","by":{"1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Georgia v. McCollum","cite":"505 U.S. 42","y":1992,"id":"1991/91-372","sc":1,"sum":"In a majority opinion authored by Justice Harry A. Blackmun, the Court found that the exercise of peremptory challenges in a racially discriminatory manner not only violates the rights of potential jurors, but also undermines the integrity of the judicial system.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gideon v. Wainwright","cite":"372 U.S. 335","y":1963,"id":"1962/155","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Sixth and Fourteenth Amendments guarantee a right of legal counsel to anyone accused of a crime.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"22","2":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gilbert v. California","cite":"388 U.S. 263","y":1967,"id":"1966/223","sc":1,"sum":"A case holding that the Fifth Amendment’s protection against self-incrimination does not prevent the taking of handwriting exemplars that identify physical characteristics in the absence of counsel.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Glasser v. United States","cite":"315 U.S. 60","y":1942,"id":"1940-1955/30","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held (1) a defense lawyer’s conflict of interest arising from a simultaneous representation of co-defendants violates Sixth Amendment guarantee of assistance of counsel, and (2) the exclusion of women (other than members of the League of Women Voters who had taken a jury training class) from the jury pool violates Sixth Amendment guarantee of an impartial jury consisting of a fair cross-section of the community.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"23, 26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Grier v. State","cite":"275 Ga. 430","y":2002,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ga./275/430/","by":{"1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Grunewald v. United States","cite":"353 U.S. 391","y":1957,"id":"1956/183","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"If the main objective of the conspiracy was to obtain the \"no prosecution\" rulings, petitioners' prosecution was barred by the three-year statute of limitations, since no agreement to conceal the conspiracy after its accomplishment was shown or can be implied on the record in this case to have been a part of the conspiracy.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Haas v. Henkel","cite":"216 U.S. 462","y":1910,"id":"scdb/1909-100","sc":1,"sum":"Notwithstanding the hardship necessarily entailed upon the accused in being tried in a district other than that in which he resides, there is no principle of constitutional law that entitles him to be tried in the place of his residence.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"30","2":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"Holmes v. Oregon & California R. Co.","cite":"9 F. 229","y":1881,"court":"C.C.D. Or.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./9/229/","op":1,"ar":1,"sum":"Recognizing that a judgment of acquittal from the wrong county would not bar a subsequent prosecution in the proper county.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Houston Community College System v. Wilson","cite":"142 S. Ct. 1253","y":2022,"id":"2021/20-804","sc":1,"sum":"Respondent Wilson does not possess an actionable First Amendment claim arising from his purely verbal censure by the Board of Trustees of the Houston Community College System.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"1":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kahler v. Kansas","cite":"140 S. Ct. 1021","y":2020,"id":"2019/18-6135","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that it does not violate the Eighth and Fourteenth Amendments for a state to abolish the insanity defense.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Klopfer v. North Carolina","cite":"386 U.S. 213","y":1967,"id":"1966/100","sc":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that indefinitely suspending a trial violates a defendant’s right to a speedy trial. The Court also held that the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment applies the Sixth Amendment to the states. The Court noted that almost every state has rejected the Supreme Court of North Carolina’s reasoning.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"Massiah v. United States","cite":"377 U.S. 201","y":1964,"id":"1963/199","sc":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that incriminating statements deliberately elicited by federal agents in the absence of counsel after the proceeding has begun violate the Sixth Amendment. The prosecution could not use these statements as evidence against Massiah. Justice Byron R. White dissented, arguing that the statements should be admitted because they were voluntarily made and not coerced.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Methard v. State","cite":"19 Ohio St. 363","y":1869,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ohio%20St./19/363/","op":1,"by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"People v. Anderson","cite":"47 Cal. 4th 92","y":2009,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Cal.%204th/47/92/","by":{"1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Peña-Rodriguez v. Colorado","cite":"580 U.S. 206","y":2017,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/580/206/","sc":1,"op":2,"by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Platt v. Minnesota Mining & Manufacturing Co.","cite":"376 U.S. 240","y":1964,"id":"1963/113","sc":1,"sum":"Respondent company, indicted for antitrust violations in an Illinois district, filed a motion to transfer the prosecution to the district of Minnesota under Rule 21 (b) of the Federal Rules of Criminal Procedure, which provides for the transfer of a multi-venue case where it would be \"in the interest of justice.\" The trial judge denied the motion, enumerating ten separate factors, including the difficulty which he …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Powell v. State","cite":"132 Fla. 659","y":1938,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Fla./132/659/","op":2,"by":{"1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Richardson v. Marsh","cite":"481 U.S. 200","y":1987,"id":"1986/85-1433","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that not only did the jury receive instructions not to consider the confession in assessing Marsh’s guilt, but also the confession was redacted so that there was no incriminating evidence regarding Marsh’s actions. Without the implication that the evidence in the confession was incriminating, Marsh’s Confrontation Clause rights were not violated.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Singer v. United States","cite":"380 U.S. 24","y":1965,"id":"1964/42","sc":1,"sum":"Federal Rule of Criminal Procedure 23 (a) sets forth a reasonable procedure governing proffered waivers of jury trials. A defendant's only constitutional right concerning the method of trial is to an impartial trial by jury. Although he may waive his right to trial by jury, Patton v. United States, 281 U. S. 276, there is no constitutional impediment to conditioning a waiver of this right on the consent of the …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Sparf v. United States","cite":"156 U.S. 51","y":1895,"id":"scdb/1894-076","sc":1,"sum":"If one of two persons accused of having together committed the crime of murder makes a voluntary confession in the presence of the other, under such circumstances that he would naturally have contradicted it if he did not assent, the confession is admissible in evidence against both.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"30","2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"State v. Hutcherson","cite":"790 S.W.2d 532","y":1990,"court":"Tenn.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.W.2d/790/532/","by":{"1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"State v. Johnson","cite":"156 N.H. 148","y":2007,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.H./156/148/","by":{"1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"State v. Schultz","cite":"329 Wis. 2d 424","y":2010,"court":"Wis. App.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Wis.%202d/329/424/","by":{"1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Taylor v. Louisiana","cite":"419 U.S. 522","y":1975,"id":"1974/73-5744","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a jury made up of a representative cross-section of the community is an essential component of the Sixth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Anderson","cite":"328 U.S. 699","y":1946,"id":"scdb/1945-028","sc":1,"sum":"1. In a criminal prosecution under § 11 of t6e Selective Training and Service Act, for refusal of the defendant to submit to induction into the armed forces, the venue is properly laid in the judicial district where the act of refusal occurred, rather than in the district where the draft board which issued the order is located.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Brennan","cite":"183 F.3d 139","y":1999,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/183/139/","by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Ewell","cite":"383 U.S. 116","y":1966,"id":"1965/29","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"The Court held that the Sixth Amendment’s right to a speedy trial did not requires dismissal of indictments against Ewell et al.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"21, 35"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Gaudin","cite":"515 U.S. 506","y":1995,"id":"1994/94-514","sc":1,"sum":"The trial judge's refusal to submit the question of \"materiality\" to the jury was unconstitutional.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"30, 36","2":"9, 14"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Gibert","cite":"25 F. Cas. 1287","y":1834,"court":"D. Mass.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Cas./25/1287/","op":1,"by":{"1":"17","2":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Hernandez","cite":"189 F.3d 785","y":1999,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/189/785/","by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Keen","cite":"26 F. Cas. 686","y":1839,"court":"C.C.D. Ind.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Cas./26/686/","op":1,"sum":"Explaining that “[a] construction of [a common law] maxim drawn from inference” and which is “somewhat doubtful” “can afford but little aid to a correct understanding of the constitution”.","sumSrc":"brief:2","by":{"1":"16","2":"15, 18"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Lee","cite":"966 F.3d 310","y":2020,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/966/310/","by":{"1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Mezzanatto","cite":"513 U.S. 196","y":1995,"id":"1994/93-1340","sc":1,"sum":"An agreement to waive the plea-statement Rules' exclusionary provisions is valid and enforceable absent some affirmative indication that the defendant entered the agreement unknowingly or involuntarily.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Tateo","cite":"377 U.S. 463","y":1964,"id":"1963/328","sc":1,"sum":"Retrial of a defendant whose conviction is set aside on collateral attack for error in the proceedings leading to conviction is not barred for double jeopardy. United States v. Ball, 163 U. S. 662, followed; Downum v. United States, 372 U. S. 734, distinguished. Pp. 463-468. 216 F. Supp. 850, reversed and remanded.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"22, 35"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. White","cite":"887 F.2d 267","y":1989,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/887/267/","by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Wade","cite":"388 U.S. 218","y":1967,"id":"1966/334","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the identifications should not be excluded if they were based on observations other than the lineup. Justice Hugo L. Black dissented in part and concurred in part, expressing that the lineup violated Wade's Fifth and Sixth Amendment rights. Justice Black would affirm the conviction, though, because the prosecution did not use evidence of the lineup at trial.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Waller v. Georgia","cite":"467 U.S. 39","y":1984,"id":"1983/83-321","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"The Court held that the Sixth Amendment right to a public trial applied to suppression hearings.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"23, 24, 35"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ward v. State","cite":"77 Ark. 19","y":1905,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ark./77/19/","by":{"1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Williams v. New York","cite":"337 U.S. 241","y":1949,"id":"scdb/1948-087","sc":1,"sum":"The Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment does not require that a person convicted after a fair trial be confronted with and permitted to cross-examine witnesses as to his prior criminal record considered by the judge in accordance with a state statute in determining what sentence to impose pursuant to broad discretion vested in him under state law-even when the jury recommends life imprisonment and the …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"12"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment IV","clauses":[["fourth-amendment","Fourth Amendment"]],"text":"The right of the people to be secure in their persons, houses, papers, and effects, against unreasonable searches and seizures, shall not be violated, and no Warrants shall issue, but upon probable cause, supported by Oath or affirmation, and particularly describing the place to be searched, and the persons or things to be seized.","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment V","clauses":[["double-jeopardy","Double Jeopardy Clause"]],"text":"No person shall be held to answer for a capital, or otherwise infamous crime, unless on a presentment or indictment of a Grand Jury, except in cases arising in the land or naval forces, or in the Militia, when in actual service in time of War or public danger; nor shall any person be subject for the same offence to be twice put in jeopardy of life or limb; nor shall be compelled in any criminal case to be a witness against himself, nor be deprived of life, liberty, or property, without due process of law; nor shall private property be taken for public use, without just compensation.","by":{"1":"21, 23"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 1832","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1832","subs":["(a)(1)"],"by":{"1":"2, 3, 4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3232","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3232","by":{"1":"2, 5, 12"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3282","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3282","subs":["(a)"],"by":{"1":"33"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3288","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3288","by":{"1":"33"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3289","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3289","by":{"1":"33"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1861","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1861","by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1863","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1863","subs":["(b)"],"by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Duncan v. Louisiana","cite":"391 U.S. 145","y":1968,"id":"1967/410","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled that the right to a jury trial in state prosecutions where sentences may be as long as two years were protected under the Sixth and Fourteenth Amendments.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jones v. United States","cite":"574 U.S. 948","y":2014,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/574/948/","sc":1,"by":{"2":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Patton v. United States","cite":"281 U.S. 276","y":1930,"id":"scdb/1929-043","sc":1,"sum":"1. After the commencement of a trial in a federal court before a jury of twelve men upon an indictment charging a crime, punishment for which may involve a penitentiary sentence, if one juror becomes incapacitated and unable to proceed further with his work as a juror, the defendant and the Government, through its official representative in charge of the case, may consent to the trial's proceeding to a finality with …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Page(s) People v. Barrett","cite":"1 Johns. 66","y":1806,"court":"N.Y. Sup. Ct.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Johns./1/66/","by":{"2":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Bell","cite":"808 F.3d 926","y":2015,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/808/926/","sum":"Describing sentencing based on acquitted conduct as “a dubious infringement” of the right “to a jury trial”.","sumSrc":"brief:2","by":{"2":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Bravo-Fernández","cite":"913 F.3d 244","y":2019,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/913/244/","sum":"Explaining that “the failure to offer any actual proof of [certain] relatively obvious jurisdictional facts has repeatedly proved fatal to criminal prosecutions” and directing entry of judgment of acquittal.","sumSrc":"brief:2","by":{"2":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Burr","cite":"25 F. Cas. 201","y":1807,"court":"C.C.D. Va.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Cas./25/201/","by":{"2":"18, 19"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Sisson","cite":"399 U.S. 267","y":1970,"id":"1969/305","sc":1,"ar":2,"sum":"Appellee was indicted for wilfully failing to report for induction as ordered by his local draft board. He moved to dismiss the indictment because (1) the involvement in Vietnam violated international law, (2) he \"reasonably believed the government's involvement in Vietnam to be illegal,\" (3) the Selective Service Act and its regulations were unconstitutional, as the local boards' procedures lacked due process, and …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Stevens","cite":"559 U.S. 460","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-769","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled that 18 U.S.C. Section 48 was unconstitutional under the Free Speech Clause of the First Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"1, 7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Weaver v. Massachusetts","cite":"137 S. Ct. 1899","y":2017,"id":"2016/16-240","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court determined that, in the context of the violation of a right to a public trial during jury selection, when the structural error was first raised via an ineffective assistance of counsel claim, the defendant must demonstrate that he suffered prejudice in order to secure a new trial.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"10"}}]};